performed PFM exercises) and a control group (10 patients did not have any exercises). Meets all criteria.

Study 2: Mobile app for treatment of stress urinary incontinence: a randomized controlled trial
- Decision: INCLUDE | Confidence: high
- Rationale: RCT in women with SUI. Intervention group used mobile app for PFMT (98.4% performed PFMT). Control group 
received postponed treatment (inactive control). Meets criteria.

Study 3: Effect of electromyographic biofeedback as an add‐on to pelvic floor muscle exercises on neuromuscular outcomes
and quality of life in postmenopausal women with stress urinary incontinence: a randomized controlled trial
- Decision: INCLUDE | Confidence: high
- Rationale: RCT in postmenopausal women with SUI. Has PFMT arms (PFME alone and PFME+BF) and a control group assessed 
at baseline and after 1 month (no treatment). Meets criteria.

Study 5: A comparison of effectiveness of biofeedback and pelvic muscle exercise treatment of stress incontinence in 
older community dwelling women
- Decision: INCLUDE | Confidence: high
- Rationale: RCT in women with stress incontinence. Has pelvic muscle exercise arm and control group. Meets all 
criteria.

Study 6: Single blind, randomised controlled trial of pelvic floor exercises, electrical stimulation, vaginal cones, and
no treatment in management of genuine stress incontinence in women
- Decision: INCLUDE | Confidence: high
- Rationale: RCT in women with genuine stress incontinence. Has pelvic floor exercise arm and untreated control group. 
Meets criteria.

Study 7: The anatomical‐functional characteristics of the pelvic floor and quality of life of women with stress urinary 
incontinence subjected to perineal exercises
- Decision: INCLUDE | Confidence: high
- Rationale: Randomized study in women with SUI. Experimental group received 16 sessions of pelvic floor exercises; 
